For any $x \in A$, $y \in B$ ($A, B \subseteq \mathbb{R}$), if there exists a unique $f(x, y)$ corresponding to the pair $(x, y)$, then $f(x, y)$ is called a bivariate function with respect to $x$ and $y$. A bivariate function $f(x, y)$ with respect to real numbers $x$ and $y$ that satisfies the following properties is defined as a generalized "distance":
(1) Non-negativity: $f(x, y) \geq 0$, with equality holding if and only if $x = y = 0$;
(2) Symmetry: $f(x, y) = f(y, x)$;
(3) Triangle inequality: $f(x, y) \leq f(x, z) + f(z, y)$ holds for any real number $z$.
Given four bivariate functions:
(Ⅰ) $f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2$;
(Ⅱ) $f(x, y) = (x - y)^2$;
(Ⅲ) $f(x, y) = \sqrt{x - y}$;
(Ⅳ) $f(x, y) = \sin(x - y)$.
Which of the above functions can be considered a generalized "distance" with respect to $x$ and $y$?
A: Ⅰ
B: Ⅱ
C: Ⅲ
D: Ⅳ

